 ],
 "literal": "Even good things and bad things which are neither, bunched clusters / of lowly-leaved calotropis, even if it is, those things that are possessed / god, 'we will not accept', without saying, in that manner / simple people and humble people, even if they go / he is duty-bound, Pāri, in the generosity of his hand.",
 "translation": "Even if they are the lowly-leaved calotropis flowers with bunched clusters, which are neither good nor bad, a deity does not refuse them saying, 'We will not accept what you have.' Just so, even if simple, humble folk go to him, Pāri is bound by duty to give with a generous hand.",
 "modern": "நல்லவையும் தீயவையும் அல்லாத, குவிந்த பூங்கொத்துகளையும் சிறிய இலைகளையும் உடைய எருக்கம் பூக்களையே படைத்தாலும், 'இவற்றை நாம் ஏற்றுக்கொள்ள மாட்டோம்' என்று கடவுள் கூற மாட்டார். அதுபோலவே, அறிவில்லாத எளியவர்களும் வறியவர்களும் தன்னிடம் வந்தாலும், அவர்களுக்கு வாரி வழங்குவதையே பாரி தன் கைகளின் கொடைத் தன்மையாகக் கடமையாகக் கொண்டுள்ளான்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is composed by the famous poet Kapilar in praise of his close friend and patron, Vēḷ Pāri. It belongs to the Puram genre, specifically the pāṭāṇ tiṇai, which celebrates a chieftain's virtues and generosity. Kapilar compares Pāri's boundless giving to the grace of a deity. He points out that when devotees offer the humble, wild calotropis (erukku) flower—which is considered neither particularly good nor bad—a god does not reject it. Similarly, Pāri does not judge the status, intelligence, or worthiness of those who approach him. Even if the most simple, weak, or impoverished people come to his gates, Pāri feels it is his absolute duty to give to them with an open hand.",
